r the stack.\n\n**Core methods.**\n- Model-assisted difference estimator.\n- D-optimal/Fisher item design.\n- Logistic (IRT-style) latent ability.\n- NNLS stacking.\n- Bagged/Laplace posterior-mean ability with tail correction. This last method gave no gain: its combined score was 0.9445, against 0.9452 for the plain stack.\n\n**Comparators.**\n- Anchor points.\n- tinyBenchmarks (anchors + IRT).\n- metabench.\n- Active evaluation acquisition.\n- Few-instance success prediction (assessors).\n- The benchmark-prediction comparison study, which is on the starter list and so permitted.\n- Random-subset mean.\n\n**Benchmarks.** Open LLM Leaderboard v2 per-item data (IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, MMLU-Pro), HELM per-item data, and ARC-Challenge.\n\n**Result-driven themes.**\n- Strong (out-of-range) targets are about twice as hard as random targets. MAE is ~0.036 vs ~0.019, and Spearman ~0.82 vs ~0.96.\n- Gains over random and anchor baselines are small in absolute MAE (~0.007–0.011) but consistent across the three variants.\n- Source-only stacking addresses extrapolation.\n- Posterior and tail calibration gave no additional benefit.\n\n## Queries (10)\n1. Anchor Points: Benchmarking Models with Much Fewer Examples (baseline)\n2. tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples (baseline)\n3. metabench sparse benchmark reasoning knowledge LLMs item response theory (baseline)\n4. Active Evaluation Acquisition for Efficient LLM Benchmarking (baseline)\n5. How Benchmark Prediction from Fewer Data Misses the Mark (result theme)\n6. predicting success of new LLM on unseen instances from few reference instances assessor (baseline)\n7. model-assisted survey estimation difference estimator generalized regression estimator (core method)\n8. D-optimal experimental design Fisher information logistic regression optimal item selection (core method)\n9. stacked generalization non-negative least squares combining predictors (architectural shift)\n10. Open LLM Leaderboard v2 IFEval BBH MMLU-Pro GPQA MuSR benchmarks (benchmark context)\n\n25\n@Article{Amalberti2005FiveSB,\n@Article{Triantafillou2017FewShotLT,\n@Article{Willcocks201025thAE,\n@Article{Yehuda2022ActiveLT,\n@Article{Vivek2023AnchorPB,\n@Article{Han2026SWESkillsBenchDA,\n@Article{Setlur2022AdversarialUR,\n@Article{Cattoni2019AGN,\n@Inproceedings{Rothermel2017DevelopmentOA,\n@Article{Ryu2020UnsupervisedFL,\n@Article{Kaartinen2012BENCHMARKINGML,\n@Article{Pencina2012PencinaEA,\n@Inproceedings{Carroll2017DonT,\n@Article{Figdor2019TheML,\n@Article{Guo2026SafeAnchorPC,\n@Article{Lee2025AgentRepairMA,\n@Inproceedings{Harris2015WorkplaceRF,\n@Article{Prüfer2026Abstract0,\n@Inproceedings{Giritharan2012IncrementalLW,\n@Inproceedings{Yuan2012JamesDM,\n@Inproceedings{Meng2016CartographyAI,\n@Inproceedings{Smith2015NetworksOM,\n@Article{Killick2017TheAA,\n@Inproceedings{Burland2019INTERNATIONALSF,\n@Article{Haman2026CanLL,","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab3199c0-ee99-4cb1-9ea1-95cee29446d4","uuid":"5b8ff7b9-5734-46be-92ec-822292251799","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:13:23.981Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"final.evidence.episodes evaluation_evidence sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_hi_delta_mae_vs_best_baseline 0.013363921531424742 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_hi_delta_mae_vs_random_mean 0.013363921531424742 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_hi_delta_score_vs_best_baseline 0.035413369961088896 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_hi_delta_score_vs_random_mean 0.035413369961088896 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_lo_delta_mae_vs_best_baseline -0.005175673983901234 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_lo_delta_mae_vs_random_mean -0.005175673983901234 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_lo_delta_score_vs_best_baseline -0.014274933501311076 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.ci_lo_delta_score_vs_random_mean -0.014274933501311076 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.combined_score 0.9313560414741708 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.control_constant_mae_delta 0.09423110758223407 sealed_final\nfinal.metric.control_permuted_m