{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018sg6QpDffF9MeH7wRGWZjX","type":"tool_result","content":"=== 01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection\n01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"Predicting a new LLM's full-benchmark accuracy from a few items is a survey-estimation problem, and a model-assisted (generalized-regression / difference) estimator should beat plain anchor-weighted means and pure IRT/latent-factor extrapolation. Fit a low-rank logistic (or SVD) latent-factor model on the source-model x item binary matrix to get item embeddings. Select k items by greedy D-optimal design over the item embeddings (so the new model's low-dimensional ability vector is well identified), with a difficulty-stratified fallback for coverage. Estimate the new model's ability from its k answers with ridge/MAP regularisation, and predict full score = (sum of observed subset outcomes + sum over unselected items of predicted P(correct)) / N, then apply a shrinkage-weighted blend with the simple subset mean and a bias correction (residual of subset observed minus model-predicted). The blend weight is chosen by leave-one-source-model-out and leave-strongest-models-out validation, so it falls back to the simple mean where the latent model extrapolates badly (new models stronger than all sources, a known failure mode of benchmark prediction). Expected: lower MAE/RMSE and higher Spearman/Kendall than random sampling, k-means anchor points, and IRT-only baselines at k in {20,50,100,200}, with the largest gains on Open LLM Leaderboard benchmarks (e.g. MMLU-Pro, BBH) and smaller or no gain under strong distribution shift (temporal / top-model holdout). Report this honestly if it does not hold.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c01_model_assisted_dopt_latent\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Load the binary model x item matrices (.jbl, pinned commit) per benchmark; split source vs target models with repeated random splits (>=20 seeds) plus a shifted split where targets are the top-scoring or newest models.\",\n  \"Implement baselines: random subset mean, stratified-by-difficulty mean, k-means anchor points with cluster-size weights, and IRT/SVD-only latent prediction.\",\n  \"Fit a low-rank latent-factor model on source models (SVD of centered matrix or logistic matrix factorization via a few alternating ridge-regularised steps); rank selected by source-model cross-validation.\",\n  \"Implement greedy D-optimal item selection: maintain M = lambda*I + sum_i v_i v_i^T over selected item embeddings and add the item maximizing the log-det gain; include a difficulty-stratified variant and a random-selection ablation.\",\n  \"Estimate the target ability vector from the k observed outcomes with ridge/MAP (np.linalg.solve on regularised normal equations or a few Newton logistic steps), predict P(correct) on unselected items.\",\n  \"Combine: difference estimator (observed subset sum + predicted remainder), residual bias correction, and shrinkage blend with the plain subset mean; choose blend weights by leave-one-source-model-out validation, fully inside the source set (no target leakage).\",\n  \"Evaluate MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall of predicted vs true full scores over target models, for k in {20,50,100,200}, with bootstrap 95% confidence intervals across target models and seeds; report paired comparisons vs baselines.\",\n  \"Run ablations (selection vs estimator contributions, latent rank, shrinkage on/off) and the shifted-split failure analysis; output results tables and a deterministic seeded entrypoint in src/program.py.\"\n ],\n \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface of program_1/src/program.py is defined by the evaluator; keep the existing entrypoint signature of the incumbe\n=== 02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator\n02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"The incumbent (c01) uses a linear low-rank latent model with clipped P(correct). Its weak spot is strong_targets (MAE ~0.039 vs ~0.018 for random_targets; Spearman ~0.77 vs ~0.96), where the target is stronger than every source and a linear model extrapolates badly (clipping bias, no saturation). Replacing/augmenting it with a logistic (2PL/multidimensional IRT-style) latent model fit by alternating Newton/IRLS on the source matrix, MAP-estimating target abilities by Newton on the k observed answers, and predicting full accuracy as the mean of sigmoid probabilities (plus a residual correction on the subset) should extrapolate more gracefully. Selecting items by a Fisher-information D-optimal criterion evaluated at abilities in the upper tail of (and slightly beyond) the source ability distribution - rather than at the source mean - should choose items that discriminate among top-performing models, reducing strong-target error and improving rank correlation. Combining the starter calibrated mean, the linear model-assisted estimate, and the logistic estimate with non-negative stacking weights fit only on source pseudo-target folds (strongest-holdout folds for strong_targets) should be no worse than c01 on random_targets (fallback to c01 candidates stays in the search space) and better on strong_targets, so combined_score exceeds the incumbent 0.9381.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c02_logistic_fisher_stacked\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Start from the incumbent program_1/src/program.py (same python_rpc_v1 handle protocol: reset/load_source/select/predict with the exact documented response dicts); keep all existing designs, linear latent fit, calibrated-mean starter, and the source-only pseudo-target validation as fallbacks so alpha=0 / starter behaviour remains reachable.\",\n  \"Add fit_logistic_latent(S, r): rank-r logistic latent model logit P_mi = b_i + a_i . theta_m fit on the source matrix by a few alternating L2-regularised Newton/IRLS sweeps (items given theta, then models given items), initialised from the SVD of the centred matrix; cap sweeps (e.g. <=15) and rank in {1,2,3,5} to keep CPU time of seconds per episode; use float64 and clip logits to [-12,12].\",\n  \"Add predict_full_score_logistic(fit, idx, Y, r, tau): MAP-estimate each target theta by Newton on its k answers with a Gaussian prior (precision grid tau), compute sigmoid probabilities on all n items, return plain (observed sum + predicted sum over unselected)/n and a residual-corrected variant (add (n-k)*mean(Y - P_sel)), clipped to [0,1].\",\n  \"Add select_items_fisher_dopt(fit, r, n_pick, chosen, theta_ref): greedy D-optimal design over Fisher information matrices I_i = p_i(1-p_i) a_i a_i^T (augmented with the intercept direction if useful) evaluated at one or several reference abilities theta_ref drawn from the top quantile of source abilities extended by a margin (e.g. top 10% mean plus 0..1 source std along the first factor); use rank-one Sherman-Morrison gains with a final np.linalg.slogdet check. Add hybrid designs mixing stratified or linear D-opt halves with Fisher-D-opt halves; keep the budget at exactly k unique items.\",\n  \"Add stack_weights(...): on source pseudo-target folds (random 5-fold for random_targets; strongest 20% and 10% holdout for strong_targets, as in the incumbent) collect the starter estimate A, linear estimate B_lin and logistic estimate B_log for each candidate design/rank/tau, and fit non-negative weights summing to ~1 with scipy.optimize.n\n=== 03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction\n03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"The remaining error of the incumbent (logistic latent + Fisher D-opt + NNLS stacking) is concentrated in strong_targets (MAE about 2x that of random_targets, lower Spearman): MAP ability estimates under a prior fit on the source ability distribution shrink toward the source mean, and a single-rank point fit has high variance when the target lies beyond the source ability range. We hypothesize that (1) a posterior-MEAN prediction (Gauss-Hermite / Laplace averaging of item probabilities over the ability posterior) with prior strength chosen on strongest-holdout source pseudo-targets, (2) bagging logistic latent models over several ranks and source-model bootstrap resamples, and (3) a monotone residual correction of the final predicted score fitted only on strongest-holdout pseudo-target folds (and shrunk to identity, with fallback to the incumbent on any failure) will reduce shrinkage bias for strong targets without hurting random targets, raising combined_score above the incumbent, mainly via lower mae_strong_targets and higher spearman_strong_targets.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c03_tail_calibrated_posterior_logistic\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Start from program_1/src/program.py (incumbent) as a strict superset; keep the incumbent estimator as a fallback path wrapped in try/except so the output is never worse than a failure.\",\n  \"Add fit_logistic_latent_bagged: fit the existing logistic latent model for ranks r in {2,3,4,6} (clipped to feasible rank) on B bootstrap resamples of source models (B about 4-8, fixed seeds); store item parameters per bag. Keep CPU cost within the existing per-episode budget by warm-starting from the full-source fit.\",\n  \"Add posterior-mean target prediction: for each bag, compute the Laplace posterior (mean = MAP, covariance = inverse of Fisher info + prior precision) of the target latent vector from the revealed answers, then predict item probabilities by averaging sigmoid over Gauss-Hermite / sampled latent draws (probit-style approximation sigmoid(m/sqrt(1+pi*v/8)) acceptable); predicted full score = revealed accuracy-weighted mix of observed items and posterior-mean probabilities on unrevealed items; average across bags.\",\n  \"Select prior precision lambda from a small grid by leave-strongest-out pseudo-target folds on source models only (hold out top-quantile source models by overall accuracy, reveal their answers on the selected items, score MAE); use the minimizer for the real target, with a weak-prior default if the grid fails.\",\n  \"Fit residual correction: using the same strongest-holdout pseudo-target predictions, fit an isotonic-free linear map y_true ~ a + b*y_pred with ridge shrinkage of (a,b) toward (0,1); apply it only when predicted score exceeds the source median accuracy (so random/mid targets are untouched), clip to [0,1]; add the corrected predictor as a fourth candidate to the existing NNLS stack (scipy.optimize.nnls) fit on source pseudo-target folds, retaining the incumbent estimators as other candidates.\",\n  \"Item selection: keep Fisher D-optimal selection at upper-tail reference abilities from the incumbent, but average the Fisher matrix over bagged item parameters so selection is stable; no changes to the candidate interface or extra diagnostics in responses.\",\n  \"Guard rails: deterministic seeding, no use of target labels beyond the revealed items, no dependence on evaluator internals; run the same code path for all benchmarks and fall back to incumbent estimate on NaN/non-f\n  391 01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1/src/program.py\n  690 02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1/src/program.py\n  956 03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1/src/program.py\n 2037 total\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab3199c0-ee99-4cb1-9ea1-95cee29446d4","uuid":"1c9489f6-7040-4974-9e86-ad682785def3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T10:13:50.602Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== 01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection\n01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"Predicting a new LLM's full-benchmark accuracy from a few items is a survey-estimation problem, and a model-assisted (generalized-regression / difference) estimator should beat plain anchor-weighted means and pure IRT/latent-factor extrapolation. Fit a low-rank logistic (or SVD) latent-factor model on the source-model x item binary matrix to get item embeddings. Select k items by greedy D-optimal design over the item embeddings (so the new model's low-dimensional ability vector is well identified), with a difficulty-stratified fallback for coverage. Estimate the new model's ability from its k answers with ridge/MAP regularisation, and predict full score = (sum of observed subset outcomes + sum over unselected items of predicted P(correct)) / N, then apply a shrinkage-weighted blend with the simple subset mean and a bias correction (residual of subset observed minus model-predicted). The blend weight is chosen by leave-one-source-model-out and leave-strongest-models-out validation, so it falls back to the simple mean where the latent model extrapolates badly (new models stronger than all sources, a known failure mode of benchmark prediction). Expected: lower MAE/RMSE and higher Spearman/Kendall than random sampling, k-means anchor points, and IRT-only baselines at k in {20,50,100,200}, with the largest gains on Open LLM Leaderboard benchmarks (e.g. MMLU-Pro, BBH) and smaller or no gain under strong distribution shift (temporal / top-model holdout). Report this honestly if it does not hold.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c01_model_assisted_dopt_latent\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Load the binary model x item matrices (.jbl, pinned commit) per benchmark; split source vs target models with repeated random splits (>=20 seeds) plus a shifted split where targets are the top-scoring or newest models.\",\n  \"Implement baselines: random subset mean, stratified-by-difficulty mean, k-means anchor points with cluster-size weights, and IRT/SVD-only latent prediction.\",\n  \"Fit a low-rank latent-factor model on source models (SVD of centered matrix or logistic matrix factorization via a few alternating ridge-regularised steps); rank selected by source-model cross-validation.\",\n  \"Implement greedy D-optimal item selection: maintain M = lambda*I + sum_i v_i v_i^T over selected item embeddings and add the item maximizing the log-det gain; include a difficulty-stratified variant and a random-selection ablation.\",\n  \"Estimate the target ability vector from the k observed outcomes with ridge/MAP (np.linalg.solve on regularised normal equations or a few Newton logistic steps), predict P(correct) on unselected items.\",\n  \"Combine: difference estimator (observed subset sum + predicted remainder), residual bias correction, and shrinkage blend with the plain subset mean; choose blend weights by leave-one-source-model-out validation, fully inside the source set (no target leakage).\",\n  \"Evaluate MAE, RMSE, Spearman and Kendall of predicted vs true full scores over target models, for k in {20,50,100,200}, with bootstrap 95% confidence intervals across target models and seeds; report paired comparisons vs baselines.\",\n  \"Run ablations (selection vs estimator contributions, latent rank, shrinkage on/off) and the shifted-split failure analysis; output results tables and a deterministic seeded entrypoint in src/program.py.\"\n ],\n \"notes_for_agent\": \"Interface of program_1/src/program.py is defined by the evaluator; keep the existing entrypoint signature of the incumbe\n=== 02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator\n02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"The incumbent (c01) uses a linear low-rank latent model with clipped P(correct). Its weak spot is strong_targets (MAE ~0.039 vs ~0.018 for random_targets; Spearman ~0.77 vs ~0.96), where the target is stronger than every source and a linear model extrapolates badly (clipping bias, no saturation). Replacing/augmenting it with a logistic (2PL/multidimensional IRT-style) latent model fit by alternating Newton/IRLS on the source matrix, MAP-estimating target abilities by Newton on the k observed answers, and predicting full accuracy as the mean of sigmoid probabilities (plus a residual correction on the subset) should extrapolate more gracefully. Selecting items by a Fisher-information D-optimal criterion evaluated at abilities in the upper tail of (and slightly beyond) the source ability distribution - rather than at the source mean - should choose items that discriminate among top-performing models, reducing strong-target error and improving rank correlation. Combining the starter calibrated mean, the linear model-assisted estimate, and the logistic estimate with non-negative stacking weights fit only on source pseudo-target folds (strongest-holdout folds for strong_targets) should be no worse than c01 on random_targets (fallback to c01 candidates stays in the search space) and better on strong_targets, so combined_score exceeds the incumbent 0.9381.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c02_logistic_fisher_stacked\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Start from the incumbent program_1/src/program.py (same python_rpc_v1 handle protocol: reset/load_source/select/predict with the exact documented response dicts); keep all existing designs, linear latent fit, calibrated-mean starter, and the source-only pseudo-target validation as fallbacks so alpha=0 / starter behaviour remains reachable.\",\n  \"Add fit_logistic_latent(S, r): rank-r logistic latent model logit P_mi = b_i + a_i . theta_m fit on the source matrix by a few alternating L2-regularised Newton/IRLS sweeps (items given theta, then models given items), initialised from the SVD of the centred matrix; cap sweeps (e.g. <=15) and rank in {1,2,3,5} to keep CPU time of seconds per episode; use float64 and clip logits to [-12,12].\",\n  \"Add predict_full_score_logistic(fit, idx, Y, r, tau): MAP-estimate each target theta by Newton on its k answers with a Gaussian prior (precision grid tau), compute sigmoid probabilities on all n items, return plain (observed sum + predicted sum over unselected)/n and a residual-corrected variant (add (n-k)*mean(Y - P_sel)), clipped to [0,1].\",\n  \"Add select_items_fisher_dopt(fit, r, n_pick, chosen, theta_ref): greedy D-optimal design over Fisher information matrices I_i = p_i(1-p_i) a_i a_i^T (augmented with the intercept direction if useful) evaluated at one or several reference abilities theta_ref drawn from the top quantile of source abilities extended by a margin (e.g. top 10% mean plus 0..1 source std along the first factor); use rank-one Sherman-Morrison gains with a final np.linalg.slogdet check. Add hybrid designs mixing stratified or linear D-opt halves with Fisher-D-opt halves; keep the budget at exactly k unique items.\",\n  \"Add stack_weights(...): on source pseudo-target folds (random 5-fold for random_targets; strongest 20% and 10% holdout for strong_targets, as in the incumbent) collect the starter estimate A, linear estimate B_lin and logistic estimate B_log for each candidate design/rank/tau, and fit non-negative weights summing to ~1 with scipy.optimize.n\n=== 03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction\n03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1:\nanalysis\ncorrect.json\nevaluation_evidence.json\nevaluation_manifest.json\nharness_evaluate_stderr.log\nharness_evaluate_stdout.log\nmetrics.json\nprogram_spec.toml\nrubric_status.json\nsrc\n\n03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1/src:\nprogram.py\n{\n \"hypothesis\": \"The remaining error of the incumbent (logistic latent + Fisher D-opt + NNLS stacking) is concentrated in strong_targets (MAE about 2x that of random_targets, lower Spearman): MAP ability estimates under a prior fit on the source ability distribution shrink toward the source mean, and a single-rank point fit has high variance when the target lies beyond the source ability range. We hypothesize that (1) a posterior-MEAN prediction (Gauss-Hermite / Laplace averaging of item probabilities over the ability posterior) with prior strength chosen on strongest-holdout source pseudo-targets, (2) bagging logistic latent models over several ranks and source-model bootstrap resamples, and (3) a monotone residual correction of the final predicted score fitted only on strongest-holdout pseudo-target folds (and shrunk to identity, with fallback to the incumbent on any failure) will reduce shrinkage bias for strong targets without hurting random targets, raising combined_score above the incumbent, mainly via lower mae_strong_targets and higher spearman_strong_targets.\",\n \"idea_id\": \"c03_tail_calibrated_posterior_logistic\",\n \"implementation_plan\": [\n  \"Start from program_1/src/program.py (incumbent) as a strict superset; keep the incumbent estimator as a fallback path wrapped in try/except so the output is never worse than a failure.\",\n  \"Add fit_logistic_latent_bagged: fit the existing logistic latent model for ranks r in {2,3,4,6} (clipped to feasible rank) on B bootstrap resamples of source models (B about 4-8, fixed seeds); store item parameters per bag. Keep CPU cost within the existing per-episode budget by warm-starting from the full-source fit.\",\n  \"Add posterior-mean target prediction: for each bag, compute the Laplace posterior (mean = MAP, covariance = inverse of Fisher info + prior precision) of the target latent vector from the revealed answers, then predict item probabilities by averaging sigmoid over Gauss-Hermite / sampled latent draws (probit-style approximation sigmoid(m/sqrt(1+pi*v/8)) acceptable); predicted full score = revealed accuracy-weighted mix of observed items and posterior-mean probabilities on unrevealed items; average across bags.\",\n  \"Select prior precision lambda from a small grid by leave-strongest-out pseudo-target folds on source models only (hold out top-quantile source models by overall accuracy, reveal their answers on the selected items, score MAE); use the minimizer for the real target, with a weak-prior default if the grid fails.\",\n  \"Fit residual correction: using the same strongest-holdout pseudo-target predictions, fit an isotonic-free linear map y_true ~ a + b*y_pred with ridge shrinkage of (a,b) toward (0,1); apply it only when predicted score exceeds the source median accuracy (so random/mid targets are untouched), clip to [0,1]; add the corrected predictor as a fourth candidate to the existing NNLS stack (scipy.optimize.nnls) fit on source pseudo-target folds, retaining the incumbent estimators as other candidates.\",\n  \"Item selection: keep Fisher D-optimal selection at upper-tail reference abilities from the incumbent, but average the Fisher matrix over bagged item parameters so selection is stable; no changes to the candidate interface or extra diagnostics in responses.\",\n  \"Guard rails: deterministic seeding, no use of target labels beyond the revealed items, no dependence on evaluator internals; run the same code path for all benchmarks and fall back to incumbent estimate on NaN/non-f\n  391 01_model_assisted_difference_estimator_with_d_optimal_latent_space_item_selection/program_1/src/program.py\n  690 02_logistic_latent_ability_model_with_extrapolation_aware_fisher_d_optimal_items_and_stacked_estimator/program_1/src/program.py\n  956 03_tail_calibrated_posterior_mean_logistic_ability_estimator_with_bagged_ranks_and_strong_holdout_residual_correction/program_1/src/program.py\n 2037 total","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /workspace/paper","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
